#11502
"Sturdy Wooden Bridge Spanning Rapids"
"This sturdy, newly-constructed wooden bridge is made of stout, roughly-cut
tree trunks bound together with thick lengths of rope, and is more than
fourteen feet wide.  It creaks almost constantly, not to threaten collapse,
but to settle more firmly into place.  There are no rails along the sides,
just a sheer drop of about twelve feet into roiling rapids, which lightly mist
the upstream side of the bridge.  The wild river to the northeast and
southwest is bordered by a thick forest of low trees, many of them partially
submerged.  To the northwest and southeast, the bridge can be followed onto a
small, rutted road.
"
""
