#11507
"Cathedral Entrance"
"Large flagstones of rough granite make up most of the floor here, with only a
few gaps where new blocks must be lain to complete this part of the
foundation.  Long, straight logs which have been shorn of branches and sanded
smooth lay lengthwise north to south, creating a track for stones to be pulled
along.  To the west are the beginnings of a wall that will one day block any
movement to the west, but for now it is only a few feet high and has many gaps
where blocks have not been placed.  A fragile-looking stone archway,
surrounded by a wooden scaffold, rises from the low stone walls to the south,
and through it leads a small dirt road.  A similar but smaller archway lies
east, while to the north is a large open area with a bare dirt floor, on each
side of which large, load-bearing stone columns are being erected.
"
